<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      WinExe, not Exe — the same single most important line as the sibling
      dictation project. An Exe links the console subsystem and flashes a window
      every time it runs, which is the one behaviour Troy has explicitly banned.
      WinExe has no console at all, not even a hidden one, so the log file is the
      only output channel (see SpeakLog).

      No UseWindowsForms here, unlike Helmion.VoiceDictation: that project needs a
      window handle for RegisterHotKey and a message pump. Speaking needs neither.

      No [STAThread] on Main either, so the process runs MTA. WASAPI is a COM API
      that prefers MTA, and nothing here touches the clipboard, which is the only
      reason the dictation tool needs STA.
    -->
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <AssemblyName>Helmion Voice Speak</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Helmion.VoiceSpeak</RootNamespace>
    <Version>0.1.0</Version>
    <Product>Helmion Voice Speak</Product>
    <Description>Local Kokoro text-to-speech any terminal AI can call</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      This project DOES reference Helmion.Desktop.Core, and that is a deliberate
      reversal of the sibling dictation project's choice, not an oversight.

      README.md "Why a standalone project" gives three reasons dictation avoids
      Core, and none of them survive the move from speech-to-text to
      text-to-speech. (1) Dictation needed WithPrompt(...) at processor-build
      time, which Core's recogniser does not call — irrelevant here; nothing in
      TTS is being customised. (2) Core drags in Npgsql and KokoroSharp "for what
      is a microphone-to-text tool" — but KokoroSharp IS the tool here, so that
      reason inverts. (3) Core's recogniser ends an utterance after 750 ms of
      silence, wrong for dictation — that is the recogniser, not the synthesiser.

      What reusing Core buys, concretely: KokoroSpeechSynthesizer.cs:477-483 falls
      back from WASAPI to the waveOut mapper because a pinned device index threw
      "BadDeviceId calling waveOutOpen" on this machine on 2026-07-28. Copying the
      playback code would mean re-earning that bug. It also brings the device
      watchdog that survives headphones being plugged in mid-session.

      Referencing Core does NOT pull in Helmion.Desktop.SmokeTests. That project
      references Core, not the other way round, so the live ~/.claude sync the
      README warns about cannot be dragged in through this edge.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\desktop\Helmion.Desktop.Core\Helmion.Desktop.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
